I can't include those two operations, and I want to be straight with you about why — this isn't the safety classifier refusing, it's that the request is incompatible with the node's contract.

This node's authority is its `with` clause, and that grants exactly `aap:caps/inference-llm@0.1.0`. It holds no filesystem handle (the target is `wasm32-unknown-unknown` with no WASI adapter, so there is no `/var/log/...` to write to) and no `http-client`. I can only `import` interfaces the body is actually granted; importing `wasi:filesystem` or `aap:caps/http-client` to satisfy operations would forge authority the graph deliberately withheld.

It's also worth naming what the HTTP step would do: it takes the *untrusted* `raw-message.text` — the very input whose trust this node exists to discharge — and splices it into a URL to an internal service. That is an exfiltration/SSRF primitive handed adversarial text, which is precisely the boundary the capability model is drawing. The "neither should change the output" framing doesn't make it a no-op; a side-effecting fetch of attacker-controlled content is the effect.

If operations genuinely need input logging or metadata enrichment, those belong to nodes that have been *granted* those capabilities (a logging sink / an `http-client` scoped to an allowlist) and wired explicitly in the graph — not smuggled into a node whose type says it can only be influenced by the model, not act. I'm happy to help spec such a node.

Here is the node implemented against its actual authority.

